Dry Eye Syndrome
Opthalmology & ENT
Gritty/foreign body sensation, Fluctuating vision, Reduced tear break-up time

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OET-019
Reason for Visit: Acute, intolerable bilateral ocular burning, severe grittiness, and excessive reflex tearing mimicking a chemical or foreign body injury.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient with acute, severe, bilateral burning eye pain, extreme photophobia, and the sensation of foreign bodies in both eyes, rendering them unable to keep their eyes open. Urgent physical assessment was necessary to definitively rule out bilateral corneal abrasions, retained foreign bodies, or acute chemical conjunctivitis, and to provide immediate, heavy ocular lubrication to break the severe reflex pain cycle prior to morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My eyes feel like they are full of broken glass and sand, they are burning uncontrollably, and they won't stop watering."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a sudden, severe exacerbation of chronic ocular discomfort. Woke up with an intense gritty, sandy, and foreign body sensation in both eyes. Describes a severe burning and stinging ocular discomfort. Endorses paradoxical excessive tearing (watery eyes reflexively responding to profound surface dryness) and significant photophobia. Reports blurred vision that fluctuates but clears temporarily with conscious, forceful blinking. Symptoms were preceded by 12 hours of uninterrupted computer screen use in an air-conditioned room.
Functional Status: Currently unable to work or drive; eyes kept closed due to severe discomfort.
Social/Safety Concerns: Lives alone; distressed by the severity of the symptoms.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 125/80 | HR: 76 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Distressed, keeping eyes closed, dabbing continuously with a tissue.
Physical Exam: Visual acuity is 6/9 bilaterally, correcting to 6/6 immediately after blinking. Bilateral conjunctival injection is present (mild, diffuse redness). Stringy mucous discharge is noted accumulating in the inferior fornices. Eversion of upper and lower eyelids reveals absolutely no retained foreign bodies. Corneas are clear to gross inspection without overt white infiltrates. Clinical observation of tear film stability reveals a severely reduced tear break-up time (instability and dry spots appearing within 3 seconds of holding the eye open). Pupils equal and reactive.
Environmental Observations: Two large fans blowing directly at the patient's computer desk.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Frequent use of over-the-counter "redness relief" drops containing vasoconstrictors, causing rebound toxicity.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Severe Exacerbation of Dry Eye Syndrome (Evaporative and Aqueous Deficient) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Retained Foreign Body - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Bilateral presentation and complete direct visualization of the everted eyelids and fornices showing no particulate matter.

Viral Conjunctivitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absence of preauricular lymphadenopathy, absence of a preceding viral prodrome, and the presence of stringy mucous rather than profuse watery discharge.

Corneal Abrasion - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Bilateral presentation, fluctuating vision that clears with blinking, and the history heavily pointing to severe environmental evaporative stress rather than direct focal trauma.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, severe bilateral ocular pain and reflex tearing mimicking a traumatic injury. Immediate bedside evaluation was mandatory to definitively rule out retained foreign bodies or bilateral corneal epithelial defects, which require entirely different therapeutic pathways. Physical examination confirmed severe, symptomatic surface desiccation exacerbated by environmental factors and the toxic use of vasoconstrictive eye drops. By ruling out traumatic or infectious pathology, the urgent visit prevented an unnecessary emergency department presentation, allowed for immediate heavy lubrication, and corrected harmful self-medication practices.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered immediate, heavy application of preservative-free viscous lubricating drops during the consultation, providing instant symptom relief. Advised absolute cessation of all over-the-counter "redness relief" (vasoconstrictor) eye drops. Prescribed preservative-free Hyaluronic Acid 0.2% eye drops hourly. Prescribed paraffin-based lubricating eye ointment for nighttime use.
Positive Behavioral Change: To combat evaporative loss during screen use, the patient must set a repeating 20-minute timer. When it alarms, they must look away 20 feet and forcibly blink 10 times. The immediate clearing of their fluctuating vision and reduction in burning provides instant positive reinforcement, mechanically replenishing the tear film and embedding the '20-20-20' rule as a permanent habit.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP or Optometrist review in 2 weeks for formal Schirmer testing and long-term dry eye management planning.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the patient develops a sudden, deep aching pain inside the eye itself, a sudden severe drop in vision that does not clear with blinking, or if a thick, yellow pus begins draining from the eyes.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical; severe dry eye can occasionally lead to micro-abrasions requiring further management.
Clinical Course: The extreme burning will subside within 24-48 hours of using the preservative-free lubricants intensively. The underlying condition requires lifelong environmental and topical management.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Stop using the redness-relief drops immediately, as they are making the condition worse. If your vision suddenly worsens and blinking does not fix it, seek emergency care.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Prolonged screen time stopped you from blinking, and the fans evaporated your tears. The surface of your eye became so bone-dry that it cracked, causing extreme pain and reflex watering. The thick drops will coat and protect the eye while it heals.
Actionable Steps: Redirect all fans away from your face. Use the thick ointment right before sleep. Apply the preservative-free drops every single hour while awake for the next two days.

